The connection folder manages the WebSocket connection state and metadata for the game client, serving as the central hub for all network communication. It handles the singleton pattern for the WebSocket connection, ensuring there's only one active connection throughout the application lifecycle. This folder acts as the foundation for all real-time game interactions, coordinating with other components like game state management and user input handling to maintain seamless network connectivity.

connectionId.ts is the single rule for the id every per-connection store is keyed by: the DSL game and the not-connected state are both `dsl-mud`; the legacy `default` id canonicalizes to `dsl-mud`; custom servers keep `host:port`. dsl-mud survives (not default) because connected users, mobile and the cloud buckets already use it, so the common case moves nothing and an older build still finds its data after a rollback. Any new store keyed by connection must build its key through canonicalConnectionId, and any cloud bucket id must too; do not add a literal default fallback.

connectionIdMigration.ts folds data stored under the legacy `default` id into `dsl-mud` (localStorage keys, the connection-keyed equipment blobs, and the library/equipment IndexedDB rows); the existing dsl-mud value wins a conflict and the loser is backed up under shatteredArchive.migrationBackup.defaultToDslMud.*. It runs on EVERY start, not behind a flag, and only acts on keys still carrying the legacy id. connectionIdMigrationBoot.ts must remain main.tsx's first import: the localStorage half has to finish before equipment-store reads localStorage at import time. A new per-connection localStorage key needs a rule in KEY_RULES or its data is orphaned under the old id. Backups deliberately use a prefix that no store scans (cloud sync's getAllGlobalScriptBuckets walks localStorage by prefix), so a backup of a legacy `default` bucket is never uploaded as if it were live data; keep any new backup key out of every store's scan prefix. Tests: autoleveling-storage.ts cannot be imported under ts-jest (import.meta), so its keying is covered only through canonicalConnectionId. The IDB migration only opens databases that already exist; creating one empty would block the owner's onupgradeneeded.
